\section{Per-Layer Sparsity Constraint Analysis}
\label{app:constraint}

To test the causal role of the KL predictor's learned per-layer allocation, we evaluate two inference-time interventions at 30\% overall sparsity (seed 42, uniform allocation baseline):

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Per-layer sparsity constraint analysis. \emph{Protect late}: cap L22--L28 sparsity $\leq$30\%; \emph{Remove valley}: override L14--L18 sparsity to 30\%. Budget redistributed to maintain 30\% overall.}
\label{tab:constraint}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Condition & PPL & ARC-c & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
KL baseline (uniform) & 8.81 & 43.7 & 40.7 & 7.5 \\
Protect late layers & 9.26 & 42.4 & 40.7 & 6.5 \\
Remove valley & 8.76 & 39.3 & 44.0 & 9.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Protecting late layers (L22--L28) does not improve knowledge-intensive metrics despite reducing their sparsity from 48--57\% to $\leq$30\%, while worsening PPL by 5.2\%.
Conversely, removing the protection valley (forcing L14--L18 to 30\% instead of 14--18\%) improves MMLU by +3.3pp and GSM8K by +1.5pp at the cost of ARC-c $-$4.4pp.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Output-distribution alignment is the dominant design dimension for PPL (signal type accounts for the majority of the gap; upper bound, confounded by train--inference mismatch in the $K{=}1$ control); cross-layer coupling contributes 10--47\% on downstream tasks (3-seed $K{=}4$-corrected; commonsense 22--47\%, GSM8K 10\%); MMLU anomalously favors $K{=}4$, reflecting allocation alignment confirmed by constraint analysis (Appendices~\ref{app:k4_downstream},~\ref{app:constraint}).
A predictor ($<$1\% of base) reduces TEAL's PPL by 14.5\% via masks 46--68\% divergent from magnitude; KL outperforms TEAL on commonsense (ARC-c, WG, HellaSwag, PIQA) but underperforms on knowledge tasks (MMLU, GSM8K) across tested scales---a learned allocation trade-off, not an inherent objective limitation (Appendix~\ref{app:constraint}).
Limitations include MMLU/GSM8K regression across all tested scales, absence of wall-clock speedup, and evaluation bounded at 7--14B.

